Certainly! If you want to force a textfield in Adobe JavaScript to display columns, you can achieve this by setting the `cols` attribute of the textfield. Here's how you can do it:

```javascript
// Assuming you have a textfield named 'myTextField'
myTextField.cols = 50;
```

In this example, `myTextField` is the name of the textfield, and `cols` is the attribute that specifies the number of columns the textfield should display. Setting it to 50 means the textfield will display 50 columns (or characters) per line.

If you need to apply this in a more dynamic way, you can also set the `cols` attribute during the textfield's initialization or dynamically based on a condition:

```javascript
// Example of setting the cols attribute during initialization
var myTextField = new Textfield("myTextField");
myTextField.x = 100;
myTextField.y = 100;
myTextField.width = 200;
myTextField.cols = 50;
myTextField.commit();
```

This way, you can control the number of columns for your textfield in Adobe JavaScript effectively.

