In LaTeX tables, you can create a new line within a cell by using the \\ command. This command tells LaTeX to start a new line within the same cell. Here's an example to illustrate:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
First Name & Last Name \\ \hline
John & Doe \\ \hline
Jane & Smith \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Example of a table with a new line within a cell.}
\end{table}

In the above example, the command \\ is used after "Last Name" to start a new line in the same cell. This will result in the table having two rows under the "Last Name" column.

You can also use \\ inside a cell to create multiple lines. For instance:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
First Name & Last Name \\ \hline
John & \\ 
\multicolumn{1}{|c|}{Doe\\[2ex]} & Smith \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Example of a table with multiple lines within a cell.}
\end{table}

In the above example, the command \\[2ex] is used to add space between the lines within the cell. This will result in the "Last Name" column having two lines with space in between.

By using these commands, you can effectively create new lines within cells in LaTeX tables.

